{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-a5cee8c811a78b47", "type": "tool_result", "content": "# CodecSlime: Temporal Redundancy Compression of Neural Speech Codec via Dynamic Frame Rate\narXiv: 2506.21074\nAuthors: Hankun Wang, Yiwei Guo, Chongtian Shao, Bohan Li, Kai Yu\nYear: 2025 | Venue: \nTopic: codec_vae\nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. Introduction of CodecSlime, a plugin-style, architecture-agnostic, and fully unsupervised method that enables dynamic frame rate (DFR) for fixed-frame-rate neural speech codecs to compress temporal redundancy.\n  2. Development of ScheDFR, an inference-time technique that utilizes dynamic programming to adaptively merge similar adjacent frames based on a feature-space distortion surrogate objective.\n  3. Proposal of Melt-and-Cool, a two-stage training recipe that adapts fixed-frame-rate codec backbones to handle dynamic frame rates through random downsampling post-training and optimal scheduling fine-tuning.\n  4. Demonstration of significant reconstruction quality improvements, achieving up to a 32% relative Word Error Rate reduction at 40 Hz compared to fixed-frame-rate baselines with similar bitrates.\n  5. Enabling of flexible trade-offs between reconstruction quality and bitrate, allowing a single trained model to support and outperform fixed-frame-rate models at multiple inference frame rates.\n\nTags: 压缩/量化-VQ, 压缩/量化-FSQ, 压缩/监督-无监督, 压缩/语义声学-声学", "is_error": false}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "89fad903-2b86-4ac0-bb38-18cb15a4f47a", "uuid": "0a5ec548-a27b-4d0d-a511-167299925620", "timestamp": "2026-08-12T04:10:29.123Z", "tool_use_result": {"stdout": "# CodecSlime: Temporal Redundancy Compression of Neural Speech Codec via Dynamic Frame Rate\narXiv: 2506.21074\nAuthors: Hankun Wang, Yiwei Guo, Chongtian Shao, Bohan Li, Kai Yu\nYear: 2025 | Venue: \nTopic: codec_vae\nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. Introduction of CodecSlime, a plugin-style, architecture-agnostic, and fully unsupervised method that enables dynamic frame rate (DFR) for fixed-frame-rate neural speech codecs to compress temporal redundancy.\n  2. Development of ScheDFR, an inference-time technique that utilizes dynamic programming to adaptively merge similar adjacent frames based on a feature-space distortion surrogate objective.\n  3. Proposal of Melt-and-Cool, a two-stage training recipe that adapts fixed-frame-rate codec backbones to handle dynamic frame rates through random downsampling post-training and optimal scheduling fine-tuning.\n  4. Demonstration of significant reconstruction quality improvements, achieving up to a 32% relative Word Error Rate reduction at 40 Hz compared to fixed-frame-rate baselines with similar bitrates.\n  5. Enabling of flexible trade-offs between reconstruction quality and bitrate, allowing a single trained model to support and outperform fixed-frame-rate models at multiple inference frame rates.\n\nTags: 压缩/量化-VQ, 压缩/量化-FSQ, 压缩/监督-无监督, 压缩/语义声学-声学", "stderr": "", "interrupted": false, "isImage": false, "noOutputExpected": false}}
{"type": "user", "message": {"role": "user", "content": [{"tool_use_id": "chatcmpl-tool-865242f6b02246b2", "type": "tool_result", "content": "# BigCodec: Pushing the Limits of Low-Bitrate Neural Speech Codec\narXiv: 2409.05377\nAuthors: Detai Xin, Xu Tan, Shinnosuke Takamichi, Hiroshi Saruwatari\nYear: 2024 | Venue: \nTopic: codec_vae\nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. We introduce BigCodec, a low-bitrate neural speech codec with a bitrate of 1.04 kbps that scales up the model size to 159M parameters and employs a single codebook with 8192 codes to avoid the extra complexity of multiple codebooks.\n  2. We demonstrate through comprehensive objective and subjective evaluations that BigCodec significantly outperforms previous low-bitrate codecs and achieves objective performance comparable to popular codecs operating at 4-6 times higher bitrates while delivering even better subjective perceptual quality than the ground truth.\n  3. We validate the contributions of model scale-up and specific design choices through ablation studies and evaluate BigCodec's generalization ability on unseen languages and its inference efficiency.\n\nTags: 压缩, 压缩/量化-VQ, 压缩/语义声学-声学", "is_error": false}]}, "parent_tool_use_id": null, "session_id": "89fad903-2b86-4ac0-bb38-18cb15a4f47a", "uuid": "ae61f55f-1935-46e8-a3df-7966c114012f", "timestamp": "2026-08-12T04:10:29.384Z", "tool_use_result": {"stdout": "# BigCodec: Pushing the Limits of Low-Bitrate Neural Speech Codec\narXiv: 2409.05377\nAuthors: Detai Xin, Xu Tan, Shinnosuke Takamichi, Hiroshi Saruwatari\nYear: 2024 | Venue: \nTopic: codec_vae\nStatus: unread\n\nContributions:\n  1. We introduce BigCodec, a low-bitrate neural speech codec with a bitrate of 1.04 kbps that scales up the model size to 159M parameters and employs a single codebook with 8192 codes to avoid the extra complexity of multiple codebooks.\n  2. We demonstrate through comprehensive objective and subjective evaluations that BigCodec significantly outperforms previous low-bitrate codecs and achieves objective performance comparable to popular codecs operating at 4-6 times higher bitrates while delivering even better subjective perceptual quality than the ground truth.\n  3. We validate the contributions of model scale-up and specific design choices through ablation studies and evaluate BigCodec's generalization ability on unseen languages and its inference efficiency.\n\nTags: 压缩, 压缩/量化-VQ, 压缩/语义声学-声学", "stderr": "", "interrupted": false, "isImage": false, "noOutputExpected": false}}
